1
00:00:31,410 --> 00:00:36,450
Nowadays it's hard to find a laboratory that doesn't contain some type of compressed gas,

2
00:00:37,170 --> 00:00:42,690
while some laboratories use these gases in many ways to fuel heating devices like buns and

3
00:00:42,690 --> 00:00:49,810
burners, to keep materials and equipment cool, as a part of experiments and processes themselves.

4
00:00:51,170 --> 00:00:55,890
While some laboratories now pipe gases in from a central storage location,

5
00:00:55,890 --> 00:01:02,050
many labs continue to use compressed gas cylinders to store and distribute the gases that they need.

6
00:01:02,050 --> 00:01:07,250
And there's a lot of gas squeezed into one of these cylinders. For example, a cylinder full

7
00:01:07,250 --> 00:01:13,490
of compressed hydrogen contains enough gas to fill a 260 cubic foot room. Of course,

8
00:01:13,490 --> 00:01:18,850
that's why gas cylinders exist, to reduce the space that's needed to store gases.

9
00:01:20,530 --> 00:01:24,690
Obviously, the gas in a compressed cylinder is under a great deal of pressure,

10
00:01:24,690 --> 00:01:43,860
and that makes the cylinders very dangerous. There are four basic ways to store pressurized gases.

11
00:01:44,580 --> 00:01:50,260
The first is called standard compression. This is where substances such as hydrogen or helium

12
00:01:50,260 --> 00:01:56,980
are squeezed into a cylinder, but kept in their gas form. The second way to store gas is as a

13
00:01:56,980 --> 00:02:03,540
liquid. This works for gases that condense under ordinary temperatures or at pressures between 25

14
00:02:03,540 --> 00:02:08,340
and 2,500 pounds per square inch, such as carbon dioxide and propane.

15
00:02:10,180 --> 00:02:15,940
The third storage method is to dissolve a gas in a solvent. Acetylene is the only common

16
00:02:15,940 --> 00:02:20,980
gas that's stored this way because it's unstable in its pure form and must be dissolved to be

17
00:02:20,980 --> 00:02:28,900
stored. The fourth method is to compress a gas into a very cool cryogenic liquid. This is only

18
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:35,220
used for substances with boiling points below negative 238 degrees Fahrenheit, like nitrogen,

19
00:02:35,220 --> 00:02:42,340
oxygen, and argon. It's important to know how each of the gases we work with is compressed,

20
00:02:42,340 --> 00:02:48,340
and as a result, how it behaves. We also need to know what precautions to take to handle each gas

21
00:02:48,340 --> 00:02:55,460
safely. One of the first things to think about is personal protective equipment. For instance,

22
00:02:55,460 --> 00:03:02,020
to work with cryogenics, you should use goggles, a face shield, and insulated impervious gloves.

23
00:03:02,020 --> 00:03:08,180
Without PPE, your skin could be severely burned. Prolonged exposure could even cause frostbite.

24
00:03:09,220 --> 00:03:13,220
And since compressed gas cylinders are heavy even when they're empty,

25
00:03:13,220 --> 00:03:19,380
it's a good idea to wear safety shoes too. Cryogenics have other unique problems.

26
00:03:20,340 --> 00:03:24,580
Extremely low temperatures can make valve washers brittle, and they can break away

27
00:03:24,580 --> 00:03:33,220
and cause leaks. Cold steel can fail when subjected to impact. Another problem with cryogenic liquids

28
00:03:33,220 --> 00:03:40,580
is that they produce huge volumes of gas when they vaporize. For instance, if the gas is oxygen,

29
00:03:40,580 --> 00:03:46,820
it can quickly enrich the atmosphere, increasing the chances for a fire or explosion. A leak of

30
00:03:46,820 --> 00:03:52,500
some other cryogenic gas may push breathable air out of the room, which could cause asphyxiation.

31
00:03:55,090 --> 00:03:59,490
We've all heard about another hazard that's associated with compressed gas cylinders.

32
00:03:59,490 --> 00:04:05,170
If a cylinder falls and damages its valve, the rapid release of gases could turn the cylinder

33
00:04:05,170 --> 00:04:11,330
into a rocket. So you can see why it's so important to handle compressed gas cylinders

34
00:04:11,330 --> 00:04:28,670
very carefully. How compressed gas cylinders are stored is also important. Cool, dry,

35
00:04:28,670 --> 00:04:33,790
well-ventilated spaces are best. Cylinders should be secured upright,

36
00:04:33,790 --> 00:04:37,070
strapped or chained into place to prevent falling or bumping.

37
00:04:37,870 --> 00:04:41,870
Safety caps should remain on until the cylinders are hooked up to their systems.

38
00:04:42,910 --> 00:04:48,830
Compressed gas should also be kept out of direct sunlight. Rising temperatures can increase the

39
00:04:48,830 --> 00:04:55,150
internal pressure in the cylinder. Flammable gases should be separated from oxidizers by at

40
00:04:55,230 --> 00:05:02,910
least 20 feet or by a wall. Any mixture of these gases could be dangerous. Flammables and oxidizers

41
00:05:02,910 --> 00:05:08,030
also need to be kept away from live electrical equipment and other potential sparking sources.

42
00:05:09,310 --> 00:05:13,070
And smoking is never permitted in cylinder storage areas.

43
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:19,600
Being able to easily see what a gas cylinder contains is important too.

44
00:05:20,640 --> 00:05:26,240
Never tamper with a cylinder's identifying labels or stencils. These are the only ways to really

45
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:32,400
tell what gases are inside. You can't rely on a cylinder's paint color since different

46
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:38,480
manufacturers use different color schemes. You need to know what the shelf life of your

47
00:05:38,480 --> 00:05:44,800
gas cylinders is as well. Cylinders containing corrosive gases shouldn't be stored for more

48
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:50,240
than six months. The same goes for substances that might undergo hazardous polymerization.

49
00:05:51,440 --> 00:05:56,000
Containers of ethylene oxide should never be stored for more than three months unless

50
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:11,840
they're refrigerated. How to handle a compressed gas cylinder when you're moving it and hooking it

51
00:06:11,840 --> 00:06:18,640
up is something else you need to know. Never drag a cylinder across the floor. This can damage the

52
00:06:18,640 --> 00:06:26,080
walls of the cylinder or its valve. Hand rolling is also a bad idea. The cylinder can easily slip

53
00:06:26,080 --> 00:06:33,360
and fall creating all sorts of problems. Moving cylinders with a hand truck is always the safest

54
00:06:33,440 --> 00:06:40,880
course. Four-wheeled versions are the most stable. You should use service elevators or dumb waiters to

55
00:06:40,880 --> 00:06:48,720
move cylinders between floors. But riding along with a cylinder is not a good idea. An elevator

56
00:06:48,720 --> 00:06:54,400
is a small space and a leaking cylinder can quickly foul the air with nowhere for you to go.

57
00:06:55,200 --> 00:07:00,000
Instead, walk up the stairs to meet the cylinder when it arrives or have someone else

58
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:06,690
in position to accept it. Safety caps should always be in place whenever a cylinder is being

59
00:07:06,690 --> 00:07:12,130
transported even over a small distance. A cap protects the cylinder's valve in the event

60
00:07:12,130 --> 00:07:19,360
it falls or bumps into something. If a falling incident does occur, don't try to catch the

61
00:07:19,360 --> 00:07:24,960
cylinder. This may seem like strange advice when you consider the damage a broken cylinder can do

62
00:07:24,960 --> 00:07:29,680
but more people are injured while trying to catch heavy falling cylinders than when the

63
00:07:29,680 --> 00:07:36,320
cylinders shoot around out of control. You're best off when you leave safety caps on, handle

64
00:07:36,320 --> 00:07:45,630
cylinders with care and always wear safety shoes. Once a cylinder gets to its final destination,

65
00:07:45,630 --> 00:07:50,430
there are other things to consider. Every lab should be equipped with fastening systems that

66
00:07:50,430 --> 00:07:56,830
will keep cylinders upright. A rack system is often the best approach. They use clamps to keep

67
00:07:56,830 --> 00:08:04,350
the cylinders in place. The strongest clamps are anchored to the wall or a lab bench at two points.

68
00:08:04,350 --> 00:08:11,230
The cylinder should fit into the racks tightly. Clamps with thumb screws are the easiest to snug

69
00:08:11,230 --> 00:08:16,670
up. The aim is to keep cylinders from falling or knocking into one another. Any physical stress

70
00:08:16,670 --> 00:08:23,230
can lead to leaks or ruptures. Chains generally aren't as good a solution since they tend to be

71
00:08:23,230 --> 00:08:28,510
looser than straps and don't always keep a cylinder well secured. If you must use a chain,

72
00:08:28,510 --> 00:08:35,550
make sure it's as tight as possible. Some states have more rigorous requirements than others for

73
00:08:35,550 --> 00:08:40,270
securing cylinders and lab accreditation groups may also have their own guidelines.

74
00:08:41,150 --> 00:08:48,750
Ask your supervisor what laws and regulations apply to your lab. Once you have a cylinder in place,

75
00:08:48,750 --> 00:08:54,510
it's finally safe to unscrew the cap. Never force a cap off with a screwdriver or bar.

76
00:08:54,510 --> 00:09:03,060
You could accidentally open or damage the valve. One last caution. If at any time you detect a gas

77
00:09:03,060 --> 00:09:08,180
leak while you're handling a cylinder, report it to your supervisor and the equipment supplier

78
00:09:08,180 --> 00:09:14,340
immediately. Then remove the cylinder from service. This goes for rusty or corroded cylinders as well.

79
00:09:26,380 --> 00:09:31,340
To hook a cylinder into a gas delivery system, you need to know how several things work.

80
00:09:32,220 --> 00:09:37,500
The first is the cylinder's regulator. This device is crucial to controlling the flow of gas

81
00:09:37,500 --> 00:09:43,180
out of the cylinder. As we've discussed, cylinders are filled to extreme pressures.

82
00:09:43,180 --> 00:09:48,540
The task of the regulator is to reduce the delivery pressure to a safe, usable level.

83
00:09:48,540 --> 00:09:55,900
The second device is the CGA fitting. This should be permanently attached to the regulator.

84
00:09:55,900 --> 00:10:03,380
It connects to the cylinder valve to allow the gas to flow out. CGA's vary depending on the type

85
00:10:03,380 --> 00:10:10,740
of gas. Some CGA's are larger than others. Some have washers. Some have notches. Threads may be

86
00:10:10,740 --> 00:10:16,340
right or left handed, so hooking up the wrong cylinder to a system becomes very hard to do,

87
00:10:16,900 --> 00:10:23,620
which is the whole idea. This is all done because mistakes with compressed gases can be critical.

88
00:10:23,620 --> 00:10:29,300
For instance, a flammable gas mistakenly hooked into a system near an ignition source could cause

89
00:10:29,300 --> 00:10:36,820
an explosion. So never attempt to force a CGA onto a valve. If it isn't fitting correctly,

90
00:10:36,820 --> 00:10:43,060
there's a reason. The third piece of equipment you have to deal with during a hookup is the

91
00:10:43,060 --> 00:10:48,180
cylinder's pressure relief device. These are installed to prevent a rupture and violent

92
00:10:48,180 --> 00:10:54,260
release of pressure if the cylinder is exposed to extreme heat. For instance, if there's a fire in

93
00:10:54,260 --> 00:10:59,540
the lab and the cylinder heats up, the relief device will open and allow for a controlled release

94
00:10:59,540 --> 00:11:06,500
of the gas inside. While if the gas is flammable, this will add fuel to the fire. It's still

95
00:11:06,500 --> 00:11:12,740
preferable to the cylinder exploding. However, there are no relief devices on cylinders used

96
00:11:12,740 --> 00:11:19,380
to store toxic or poisonous substances. Any escape of these gases is considered too dangerous to risk.

97
00:11:20,740 --> 00:11:26,980
A last device, a flame arrestor, is often used with flammable gas cylinders. This helps to prevent

98
00:11:26,980 --> 00:11:42,740
flashback into the cylinder in case a fire develops somewhere in the system. Once you've assembled your

99
00:11:42,740 --> 00:11:48,180
gas system and have it up and running, you should check all fittings and valves for leaks. An easy

100
00:11:48,180 --> 00:11:53,780
way to do this is to cover the surfaces with a diluted soap solution. If you see any bubbles,

101
00:11:53,780 --> 00:11:59,940
you've got a problem. Let's look at what would happen if a cylinder full of compressed hydrogen

102
00:11:59,940 --> 00:12:05,940
develops a leak at the valve. The pressure in the cylinder forces out the gas, pushing it great

103
00:12:05,940 --> 00:12:12,260
distances. It can flow over bench tops, past electrical devices and heat sources, and in its

104
00:12:12,260 --> 00:12:19,220
travels find a source of ignition, a spark or open flame. There are three things going on here.

105
00:12:19,220 --> 00:12:24,420
A leak in the system, the wide dispersal of the gas caused by the pressure in the cylinder,

106
00:12:24,420 --> 00:12:29,700
and finally, the specific hazards of the substance itself. In this case, it's flammability.

107
00:12:32,190 --> 00:12:36,110
While most leak repairs are simple, you still need to take precautions.

108
00:12:37,470 --> 00:12:41,790
First and foremost, you should follow your facility's emergency plan and the directions

109
00:12:41,790 --> 00:12:46,190
on the safety data sheets for the gases that you're working with. But there are also some

110
00:12:46,190 --> 00:12:52,990
things that you should do for any leaks. Immediately alert other employees in the area.

111
00:12:54,430 --> 00:12:57,550
Depending on the size of the leak and the gas that's involved,

112
00:12:57,550 --> 00:13:04,190
you may have to evacuate the lab. Make sure you know both the primary and secondary evacuation

113
00:13:04,190 --> 00:13:10,990
routes. Once you've made sure that everyone is safe, notify your supervisor so that the needed

114
00:13:10,990 --> 00:13:22,620
repairs can be made. Using gas cylinders is only complicated when you don't do your homework.

115
00:13:23,180 --> 00:13:29,340
Let's review. Always be aware of the properties and hazards of the gases you work with.

116
00:13:31,100 --> 00:13:34,940
Know how to safely store and transport compressed gas cylinders.

117
00:13:36,780 --> 00:13:43,180
Remember the rules for using regulators, CGAs and pressure relief devices, as well as how they work.

118
00:13:43,180 --> 00:13:49,180
Know the procedures you should follow to hook cylinders into gas systems correctly

119
00:13:50,700 --> 00:13:55,100
and be prepared to deal with a gas leak or other gas-related emergency.

120
00:13:57,100 --> 00:14:04,220
Most accidents involving compressed gases should never happen. With some preparation and foresight,

121
00:14:04,220 --> 00:14:10,380
you can keep the gases that you use under control and your lab safe and incident-free.
